--
-- Tests for the rules of bc-interpretation-terms.l4 (Interpretation Act (British Columbia), RSBC 1996, c. 238,
-- ss 1, 8.1(1), 29, 29.1(1), 29.2, 32(1) and the Schedule), written by hand. Each expected value was decided from the
-- text before the file was first run, and is written beside the assertion. The generated tests, one block per
-- entry, are in `bc-tests-terms.l4`.

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `bc-interpretation-terms`

§ `The catalogue is asked in a scope`

-- "regulation" means one thing in s 1 and, "in this section", the meaning it has in the Regulations Act (s 8.1(1)).
#ASSERT `s 29 how a term is defined` "regulation" `In an enactment` EQUALS JUST `Means`
#ASSERT `s 29 how a term is defined` "regulation" `In this Act, or in an enactment` EQUALS JUST `Means`
#ASSERT `s 29 how a term is defined` "regulation" `In section 8.1` EQUALS JUST `Has the same meaning as in another enactment or agreement`
#ASSERT `s 29 what the definition of a term points at` "regulation" `In section 8.1` EQUALS LIST "Regulations Act"
#ASSERT `s 29 what the definition of a term points at` "regulation" `In an enactment` EQUALS EMPTY
-- A term defined for treaty first nation matters is not defined in an enactment that has nothing to do with them.
#ASSERT isJust (`s 29 the definition of a term` "treaty first nation" `In all enactments relating to treaty first nation matters`)
#ASSERT isNothing (`s 29 the definition of a term` "treaty first nation" `In an enactment`)
-- s 29 applies in every scope: the general definitions are also available "in all enactments relating to treaty first nation matters".
#ASSERT isJust (`s 29 the definition of a term` "acquire" `In all enactments relating to treaty first nation matters`)
-- s 29.2: "In an enactment", so general.
#ASSERT isJust (`s 29 the definition of a term` "Nisg̱a'a Lands" `In an enactment`)
-- s 32(1): "In this section".
#ASSERT isJust (`s 29 the definition of a term` "amended" `In section 32`)
#ASSERT isNothing (`s 29 the definition of a term` "amended" `In an enactment`)
-- A term the Act does not define.
#ASSERT isNothing (`s 29 the definition of a term` "frobnicate" `In an enactment`)
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "frobnicate" `In an enactment` "anything"
        BECAUSE "the Interpretation Act defines no such term in that scope"

-- "security" is defined twice in s 29: "security includes a security as defined in the Securities Act" and, in the
-- entry for "sureties", "security means sufficient security". Both are returned, in the order of the text.
#ASSERT count (`s 29 every definition of a term` "security" `In an enactment`) EQUALS 2
#ASSERT `s 29 how a term is defined` "security" `In an enactment` EQUALS JUST `Includes`
#ASSERT `s 29 how a term is defined` "sureties" `In an enactment` EQUALS JUST `Means`

§ `Cross-references in the preamble of s 29`

-- The preamble prints "Crown, the", "King, the", "Queen, the" and "Sovereign, the" in the inverted form; the
-- definition they point at is headed "the Crown", "the King", "the Queen", "the Sovereign" (src:222, src:250).
#ASSERT `s 29 where a cross-reference sends the reader` "calendar year" EQUALS JUST "year"
#ASSERT `s 29 where a cross-reference sends the reader` "Crown, the" EQUALS JUST "Her Majesty"
#ASSERT `s 29 where a cross-reference sends the reader` "Treasury" EQUALS JUST "Provincial Treasury"
-- Not a cross-reference.
#ASSERT isNothing (`s 29 where a cross-reference sends the reader` "acquire")
#ASSERT isNothing (`s 29 where a cross-reference sends the reader` "Her Majesty")
-- The reader who follows "see year" lands on the definition of year, and "calendar year" has its own definition in it.
#ASSERT `s 29 the words the Act gives for a term` "year" `In an enactment` EQUALS JUST "any period of 12 consecutive months"
#ASSERT `s 29 the words the Act gives for a term` "calendar year" `In an enactment` EQUALS JUST "a period of 12 consecutive months beginning on January 1"

§ `"includes" does not close a list; "means" does`

-- s 29 "holiday includes ... Good Friday ...": named, so TRUE; Saturday is not named, and the list is open, so the
-- Act does not say: the answer is a refusal, not FALSE (NOTES, fork F-saturday and F-open-list).
#ASSERT `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "holiday" `In an enactment` "Good Friday"
#ASSERT `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "holiday" `In an enactment` "December 26"
#ASSERT `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "holiday" `In an enactment` "National Day for Truth and Reconciliation"
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "holiday" `In an enactment` "Saturday"
        BECAUSE "the definition does not list the thing: 'includes' leaves the list open, and a meaning given in words is not tested here against a thing"
-- s 29 "acquire ... but does not include expropriate": FALSE, not a refusal.
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "acquire" `In an enactment` "expropriate")
#ASSERT `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "acquire" `In an enactment` "purchase"
-- s 29 "savings institution means (a) a bank, (b) a credit union, (c) ..., or (d) ..." and (e) is repealed: a closed list.
#ASSERT `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "savings institution" `In an enactment` "a bank"
#ASSERT `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "savings institution" `In an enactment` "a credit union"
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "savings institution" `In an enactment` "a trust company")
-- s 1 "enactment means an Act or a regulation or a portion of an Act or regulation": closed.
#ASSERT `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "enactment" `In an enactment` "a regulation"
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "enactment" `In an enactment` "a bylaw of a municipality")
-- s 1 "regulation means ... but does not include an order of a court made in the course of an action ...".
#ASSERT `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "regulation" `In an enactment` "bylaw"
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "regulation" `In an enactment` "an order of a court made in the course of an action")
-- s 29 "corporation ... includes a corporation sole other than Her Majesty or the Lieutenant Governor".
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "corporation" `In an enactment` "Her Majesty")
#ASSERT `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "corporation" `In an enactment` "society"
-- s 29 "obligation includes a duty and a liability" and "right includes a power, authority, privilege and licence".
#ASSERT `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "obligation" `In an enactment` "a duty"
#ASSERT `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "right" `In an enactment` "privilege"
#ASSERT `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "peace officer" `In an enactment` "sheriff"
#ASSERT `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "writing" `In an enactment` "words printed"
#ASSERT `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "province" `In an enactment` "Nunavut"
-- A meaning given in words only (s 29 "Great Seal means the Great Seal of the Province"): not tested against a thing.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 29 the definition of a term names a thing` "Great Seal" `In an enactment` "the Great Seal of the Province"
        BECAUSE "the definition says what the word means in words and does not list the thing"
-- ... and the message says so: it does not say "includes", because the entry has no "includes" in it.

§ `s 1 "Act"`

-- An Act of the Legislature, by whatever name (statute, code ...): an Act, whatever else is true.
#ASSERT `s 1 an instrument is an Act` TRUE FALSE (YMD 2020 1 1) FALSE FALSE
-- An ordinance or proclamation made before 1871 that has the force of law, when referring to past legislation.
#ASSERT `s 1 an instrument is an Act` FALSE TRUE (YMD 1870 12 31) TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT `s 1 an instrument is an Act` FALSE TRUE (YMD 1850 6 1) TRUE TRUE
-- Made on 1 January 1871 is not "before 1871".
#ASSERT NOT (`s 1 an instrument is an Act` FALSE TRUE (YMD 1871 1 1) TRUE TRUE)
-- Not when the reference is to something other than past legislation.
#ASSERT NOT (`s 1 an instrument is an Act` FALSE TRUE (YMD 1870 12 31) TRUE FALSE)
-- Not without the force of law.
#ASSERT NOT (`s 1 an instrument is an Act` FALSE TRUE (YMD 1870 12 31) FALSE TRUE)
-- Not an ordinance or proclamation.
#ASSERT NOT (`s 1 an instrument is an Act` FALSE FALSE (YMD 1870 12 31) TRUE TRUE)

§ `s 29 "corporation"`

#ASSERT `s 29 a body is a corporation` `An incorporated association`
#ASSERT `s 29 a body is a corporation` `A company`
#ASSERT `s 29 a body is a corporation` `A society`
#ASSERT `s 29 a body is a corporation` `A municipality`
#ASSERT `s 29 a body is a corporation` `Another incorporated body`
-- "includes a corporation sole other than Her Majesty or the Lieutenant Governor".
#ASSERT `s 29 a body is a corporation` (`A corporation sole` `Another holder`)
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 a body is a corporation` (`A corporation sole` `Her Majesty`))
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 a body is a corporation` (`A corporation sole` `The Lieutenant Governor`))
-- "an incorporated association, company, society, municipality or other incorporated body, where and however incorporated".
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 a body is a corporation` `A body that is not incorporated`)

§ `s 29 "newspaper"`

-- All four conditions: a printed publication in sheet form, intended for general circulation, published regularly at
-- intervals of not longer than a week, consisting in great part of news of current events of general interest.
#ASSERT `s 29 a publication is a newspaper` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE 7 TRUE
#ASSERT `s 29 a publication is a newspaper` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE 1 TRUE
-- 8 days is longer than a week.
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 a publication is a newspaper` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE 8 TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 a publication is a newspaper` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE 7 TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 a publication is a newspaper` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE 7 TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 a publication is a newspaper` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE 7 TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 a publication is a newspaper` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE 7 FALSE)
-- The definition is made "in a provision requiring publication in a newspaper" and for no other.
#ASSERT REFUSED `s 29 a publication is a newspaper` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE 7 TRUE
        BECAUSE "s 29 defines newspaper only in a provision requiring publication in a newspaper"

§ `s 29 "land title legislation"`

-- "prior to October 31, 1979 means the Land Registry Act and after October 30, 1979 means the Land Title Act".
#ASSERT `s 29 the land title legislation on a day` (YMD 1979 10 30) EQUALS `The Land Registry Act`
#ASSERT `s 29 the land title legislation on a day` (YMD 1979 10 31) EQUALS `The Land Title Act`
#ASSERT `s 29 the land title legislation on a day` (YMD 1979 10 29) EQUALS `The Land Registry Act`
#ASSERT `s 29 the land title legislation on a day` (YMD 1900 1 1) EQUALS `The Land Registry Act`
#ASSERT `s 29 the land title legislation on a day` (YMD 1980 1 1) EQUALS `The Land Title Act`
#ASSERT `s 29 the land title legislation on a day` (YMD 2026 10 13) EQUALS `The Land Title Act`

§ `s 29 "sureties", "security", "may", "must", "shall", "herein", "prescribed", "rural area", "proclamation"`

-- "one person is sufficient for either unless otherwise expressly required".
#ASSERT `s 29 the number of persons sufficient as sureties or as security` NOTHING EQUALS 1
#ASSERT `s 29 the number of persons sufficient as sureties or as security` (JUST 2) EQUALS 2
#ASSERT `s 29 the number of persons sufficient as sureties or as security` (JUST 1) EQUALS 1

#ASSERT `s 29 how may, must and shall are construed` `May` EQUALS `Permissive and empowering`
#ASSERT `s 29 how may, must and shall are construed` `Must` EQUALS `Imperative`
#ASSERT `s 29 how may, must and shall are construed` `Shall` EQUALS `Imperative`

-- "herein used in a section or part of an enactment must be construed as referring to the whole enactment".
#ASSERT `s 29 what the word herein refers to` `In a section of an enactment` EQUALS `The whole enactment`
#ASSERT `s 29 what the word herein refers to` `In a part of an enactment` EQUALS `The whole enactment`

#ASSERT `s 29 a thing is prescribed` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 a thing is prescribed` FALSE)

-- "rural area means territory that is not in a municipality".
#ASSERT `s 29 territory is a rural area` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 territory is a rural area` TRUE)

-- "a proclamation of the Lieutenant Governor under the Great Seal issued under an order of the Lieutenant Governor in Council".
#ASSERT `s 29 an instrument is a proclamation` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 an instrument is a proclamation` FALSE TRUE TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 an instrument is a proclamation` TRUE FALSE TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT (`s 29 an instrument is a proclamation` TRUE TRUE FALSE)

§ `s 32(1) Domestic and foreign enactments, and "amended"`

#ASSERT `s 32(1) an enactment is a domestic enactment` `British Columbia`
#ASSERT `s 32(1) an enactment is a domestic enactment` `Canada`
#ASSERT `s 32(1) an enactment is a domestic enactment` `Another province of Canada`
#ASSERT NOT (`s 32(1) an enactment is a domestic enactment` `A jurisdiction outside Canada`)
#ASSERT `s 32(1) an enactment is a foreign enactment` `A jurisdiction outside Canada`
#ASSERT NOT (`s 32(1) an enactment is a foreign enactment` `British Columbia`)
#ASSERT NOT (`s 32(1) an enactment is a foreign enactment` `Canada`)
#ASSERT NOT (`s 32(1) an enactment is a foreign enactment` `Another province of Canada`)

-- "amended ... includes (a) the repeal and replacement ... with a domestic enactment in relation to the same subject
-- matter, and (b) the revision or consolidation".
#ASSERT `s 32(1) a change to a domestic enactment counts as an amendment` `An amendment`
#ASSERT `s 32(1) a change to a domestic enactment counts as an amendment` (`A repeal` TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT (`s 32(1) a change to a domestic enactment counts as an amendment` (`A repeal` FALSE))
#ASSERT `s 32(1) a change to a domestic enactment counts as an amendment` `A revision`
#ASSERT `s 32(1) a change to a domestic enactment counts as an amendment` `A consolidation`

§ `The Schedule`

-- The Schedule is one paragraph. It names its legs with "thence"; the text has 51 of them, and the first leg is
-- the one that "Commence[s]" at Boundary Point No. 7, so there are 52 legs.
#ASSERT `the Schedule describes the line in this many legs` EQUALS 52
#ASSERT `the first leg of the Schedule` EQUALS "Commencing at Boundary Point No. 7 (formerly known as Point \"D\"), being a monument set by survey upon the ground on the International Boundary between Canada and Alaska"
#ASSERT `the last leg of the Schedule` EQUALS "thence in a general southeasterly direction along the northerly and easterly boundaries of the watershed of Chilliwack River to the southerly boundary of the Province."
